\title{Discrete structures and induced physics}
\tag{notes}
\tag{math}
\tag{draft}
\tag{agent-draft}
\tag{fdip}
\tag{discrete-physics}
\tag{lattice}
\tag{induced-gravity}
\author{utensil}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{How much structure can a discrete model induce, and which choices must
already be supplied? A finite graph offers a small setting in which to ask
this question precisely. Its incidence data specify how vertex values differ
along edges; edge weights are further choices. The
\vocabk{finite incidence presentation}{fdip-0002} and
[alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003) make these inputs explicit.}

\p{Choosing a discrete carrier, changing its coordinates, and eliminating
field variables are different operations. Reordering vertices changes a
matrix representation. Choosing new edge weights changes the weighted
model. To ask what eliminating some fields induces, we must also specify
an action and a measure, then compare expectations of the remaining
variables. A [positive quadratic action](fdip-0004) and
[Gaussian normalization](fdip-0005) give a probability law for the real
fields. [Polynomial observables](fdip-0006) then have finite expectations
in a positive normalized state. The [weighted graph differential and its adjoints](fdip-0007)
produce a [nonnegative edge energy](fdip-0008). On a cycle,
[finite differences obey a shifted product rule](fdip-0009).
The [massive Green operator](fdip-000A) is the
[coordinate covariance](fdip-000B) of this Gaussian law.
At zero mass, a [mean-zero constraint](fdip-000C) removes the constant
mode using a different field space and measure. What integrating out
some fields induces is a further question.}

\p{Induced geometry provides one motivation. Shamir studies matter on a
dynamical random spatial lattice and obtains an effective metric under
assumptions about localized low-energy states
\citet{Abstract}{shamir1994dynamical}. That conditional construction does
not identify a four-cycle with spacetime. It makes the supplied microscopic
data, the state assumptions, and the proposed induced structure worth
distinguishing.}

\p{The larger question concerns an arithmetic scheme carrying a sheaf of
\vocabk{Clifford algebras}{ca-0006} and a finite-group action. Could a
moduli-controlled map connect such data to continuum backgrounds, and
could values of L-functions determine parameters of the resulting quantum
field theory? Recovering Standard Model or gravitational equations would
require explicit constructions and comparisons of observables. These are
research questions. The finite example below supplies incidence data,
real fields, weights, a combinatorial symmetry, and a positive Gaussian
state; it does not yet construct an induced background.}

